WEBVTT

00:00:00.174 --> 00:00:02.444
What if all of India
spoke just one language?

00:00:02.579 --> 00:00:06.479
India has twenty-two official languages,
and hundreds more spoken in homes,

00:00:06.479 --> 00:00:07.929
markets and villages.

00:00:08.165 --> 00:00:10.965
Even a rupee note prints its
value in fifteen of them.

00:00:10.965 --> 00:00:12.815
One language would make life simple.

00:00:12.851 --> 00:00:15.541
A farmer in Assam could
talk to a doctor in Kerala.

00:00:15.701 --> 00:00:18.251
Exams, courts and apps would
work the same everywhere.

00:00:18.387 --> 00:00:20.176
But a language is more than words.

00:00:20.322 --> 00:00:22.892
It carries songs, jokes,
proverbs and stories.

00:00:23.017 --> 00:00:26.238
Lose the language,
and those fade one grandmother at a time.

00:00:26.238 --> 00:00:27.668
And whose language would it be?

00:00:27.718 --> 00:00:30.868
Even today, many states feel
strongly about this question.

00:00:30.889 --> 00:00:33.539
Millions of people would also
have to relearn how to read,

00:00:33.769 --> 00:00:36.139
write and do business, almost overnight.

00:00:36.169 --> 00:00:38.619
India's strength is that
it speaks in many voices.

00:00:38.704 --> 00:00:40.414
So what if it spoke in just one?

